1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
Same 16-gig GPU. A standard Transformer: about 20K tokens, and it's full.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,300
ProxyFormer: seven hundred thousand.

3
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:06,950
Same length: one-fifth the memory, twelve times the speed.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,000
How?

5
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:16,204
This is ProxyFormer, a new architecture for ultra-long context and high-resolution generation.

6
00:00:16,204 --> 00:00:22,137
Its core idea: let a tiny set of 'proxy tokens' carry all the global interaction.

7
00:00:22,937 --> 00:00:29,271
In earlier work, 512 tokens were squeezed into 8 vectors and rebuilt almost losslessly.

8
00:00:29,271 --> 00:00:35,871
So the natural question: why let expensive attention run on the raw, uncompressed sequence?

9
00:00:36,671 --> 00:00:40,821
First, the problem. What makes long context expensive?

10
00:00:40,821 --> 00:00:46,821
In attention, every token talks to every other token. Eight tokens means 64 pairs.

11
00:00:46,821 --> 00:00:51,837
Double the length and the work quadruples. That's quadratic growth.

12
00:00:52,837 --> 00:00:56,404
At a million tokens, that's a trillion pairs.

13
00:00:57,404 --> 00:01:02,554
And at inference the KV cache grows linearly, eating GPU memory fast.

14
00:01:03,354 --> 00:01:06,321
ProxyFormer's idea fits one analogy.

15
00:01:06,321 --> 00:01:11,804
Picture a ten-thousand-person conference. Everyone can't talk to everyone.

16
00:01:12,604 --> 00:01:16,754
All-to-all: 24 people already means 276 conversations.

17
00:01:17,354 --> 00:01:20,854
Better: each table sends one representative.

18
00:01:20,854 --> 00:01:25,871
The rep first listens to everyone at the table. That's compression.

19
00:01:25,871 --> 00:01:30,687
Then only the reps meet with each other. Just six conversations.

20
00:01:30,687 --> 00:01:37,421
Finally each rep goes back and shares the global picture. That's decompression and injection.

21
00:01:38,221 --> 00:01:44,687
Compress every r tokens into one proxy, and global attention drops to one over r squared,

22
00:01:44,687 --> 00:01:48,454
and length-dependent memory drops to one over r.

23
00:01:48,454 --> 00:01:52,604
At a ratio of 64, attention costs one four-thousandth.

24
00:01:53,404 --> 00:01:57,037
But compression always loses something, right?

25
00:01:57,037 --> 00:02:01,387
Compress once, and whatever got dropped is gone for good.

26
00:02:01,387 --> 00:02:07,654
ProxyFormer does it differently: the fine-grained local stream is kept at every layer.

27
00:02:07,654 --> 00:02:12,871
Each layer: local features condense into proxies, micro guiding macro;

28
00:02:12,871 --> 00:02:15,437
the proxies interact globally;

29
00:02:15,437 --> 00:02:21,637
then the global view is decompressed back into the local stream, macro guiding micro.

30
00:02:21,637 --> 00:02:26,254
Meanwhile the local stream itself flows on to the next layer.

31
00:02:29,054 --> 00:02:35,321
So detail missed by one compression step is still there for the next layer to pick up.

32
00:02:36,121 --> 00:02:40,737
A few more design choices push the compression ratio further.

33
00:02:40,737 --> 00:02:47,937
First, cascades: instead of 64x in one jump, compress 4x three times, then decompress symmetrically.

34
00:02:47,937 --> 00:02:52,821
And different layers, or older history, can use different ratios.

35
00:02:53,621 --> 00:03:00,554
Second, the proxy KV cache. When generating, a new token doesn't look back at the whole history;

36
00:03:00,554 --> 00:03:05,837
it only attends to a tiny cache of proxies, cutting memory and latency.

37
00:03:05,837 --> 00:03:12,771
Third, asymmetric embeddings: history starts in a narrower space, so savings begin at layer one.

38
00:03:13,571 --> 00:03:18,054
And all of it uses the most common deep-learning operators,

39
00:03:18,054 --> 00:03:24,121
with no sparse indexers or hand-written CUDA kernels. Easy to reproduce and deploy.

40
00:03:24,921 --> 00:03:29,404
There's also an elegant property, borrowed from image VAEs.

41
00:03:30,404 --> 00:03:37,854
Raise the compression ratio by alpha: proxies get alpha times shorter, attention drops by alpha squared.

42
00:03:37,854 --> 00:03:43,004
Reinvest the savings into channels: make each proxy beta times wider.

43
00:03:43,004 --> 00:03:49,804
As long as beta stays under alpha squared, the budget doesn't grow, yet each proxy holds more.

44
00:03:50,804 --> 00:03:57,737
A virtuous cycle: compress harder, save more; stronger channels then support even higher ratios.

45
00:03:58,537 --> 00:04:03,287
Now the experiments. First: a single 16 GB GPU, batch size one.

46
00:04:04,287 --> 00:04:10,221
With ~21K tokens of history, full attention uses 15.6 GB at 1.3 steps per second.

47
00:04:10,221 --> 00:04:14,504
ProxyFormer uses 2.9 GB, and runs over ten times faster.

48
00:04:14,504 --> 00:04:21,237
Fill the card, and ProxyFormer trains on 716,800 tokens: about 35 times the trainable length.

49
00:04:22,037 --> 00:04:29,963
Second: multi-needle retrieval. Fifty passkeys hidden per document, each one queried, 5,000 lookups per length.

50
00:04:29,963 --> 00:04:37,680
This is much harder than a single-needle test: all 50 needles must be found, at lengths far beyond training.

51
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:40,847
This model was trained on a 64K window.

52
00:04:40,847 --> 00:04:47,647
Push to 128K and 256K, four times the training length: 99 to 100 percent of the needles found.

53
00:04:47,647 --> 00:04:53,647
Now push to the limit: 512K, then a million tokens, sixteen times beyond training.

54
00:04:53,647 --> 00:04:57,730
Even there, every depth still holds 92 to 95 percent.

55
00:04:57,730 --> 00:05:03,080
And no 'lost in the middle': beginning, middle, end, all equally stable.

56
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:09,547
A model trained on just 8K still tops 94% at 256K, 32 times out; beyond that it degrades.

57
00:05:10,347 --> 00:05:15,163
With compression this aggressive, does language modeling suffer?

58
00:05:15,163 --> 00:05:22,947
On WikiText-103, ProxyFormer with compressed history scores 21.01 perplexity, on par with the 21.36 baseline.

59
00:05:22,947 --> 00:05:29,013
Early image-generation runs, in both pixel and latent space, also confirm it works.

60
00:05:29,813 --> 00:05:35,429
The same chunk, compress, interact, decompress recipe isn't limited to text.

61
00:05:35,429 --> 00:05:42,229
1D text, 2D image patches, 3D video, point clouds and voxels, even higher-dimensional tensors:

62
00:05:42,229 --> 00:05:45,463
all handled by the same proxy mechanism.

63
00:05:45,463 --> 00:05:52,396
In text-to-image, the text condition talks only to compressed image proxies, so fusion is cheap.

64
00:05:53,196 --> 00:06:00,000
To be clear: these experiments prove the architecture works. They're not chasing leaderboards.

65
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,750
How far it goes with serious compute is still an open question.

66
00:06:05,750 --> 00:06:11,683
ProxyFormer's core method, the Proxy Token, has been formally filed for a patent.

67
00:06:11,683 --> 00:06:18,150
Academic and non-commercial use is free. For commercial deployment, let's talk licensing.

68
00:06:19,150 --> 00:06:28,983
As an independent researcher, the author welcomes partners with compute, funding, or real use cases to push this architecture to its limits.

69
00:06:28,983 --> 00:06:33,533
Paper, code and contact are right here. Thanks for watching.
